WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.540
Film Courage: What did FOR THE LOVE OF GEORGE
teach you about screenwriting?

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.920
Nadia Jordan: I mean it really taught me the
whole to be honest because this was my first

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.809
feature.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:14.150
It really taught me…you know it’s very
different when you write a book or a story.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:23.140
I’m really aware of how pacing is so important
and dialogue is so important and how my co-writer

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.449
has a tendency to make things really wordy
and I’m somebody who does not particularly

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:31.519
like wordy films.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:32.519
Woody Allen is great.

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:43.410
All his films are extremely wordy and so yeah
visual is very important, location is very

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:49.410
important, you have to think when you’re
writing, it needs to translate into film,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.410
right?

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.120
So it just basically taught me the whole process.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.280
How to make a movie, it all begins with a
good script and it’s so important when you

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.280
start writing.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.770
It can’t just read well, it has to translate
on the screen.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:07.100
Film Courage: What did it teach you in the
sense of completing something or the idea

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.930
of something and then the actual time and
energy it takes to do it?

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.210
You know, sort of taking away the mystique
of “Oh, I’m making a movie!”

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.990
And how exciting it sounds, but also how stressful
I’m sure it can be.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.050
Nadia Jordan: Even now people say “Well,
that’s great.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.050
You made a movie.”

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.460
And when you’re in the middle of it, it’s
like…I mean obviously none of us would be

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.520
in this industry doing this if we didn’t
enjoy it and it wasn’t something we wanted

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.520
to do.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.640
But for me there were so many struggles and
so many problems on the producing side of

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.050
it and so many curveballs like the [George
Clooney] engagement.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:51.100
And even just like financing and trying to
get a director and having producers sign on

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.110
and then drop out.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.200
It was just so much.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.460
And it really kind of taught me…I mean there
were days when just basically I wanted to

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.600
give up and thought this just isn’t worth
it.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.840
But you just get up and say “Just keep going.”

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.940
And it really just kind of made me realize
that if you’re passionate about something,

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.070
there isn’t anything to it and it’s something
you can be really proud of.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:18.620
And to now look back and think I did this
feature and it started as just this little

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:24.170
idea and I’m proud that it’s my first
feature that I’ve written (the first screenplay),

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.240
the first film I’ve produced, the film that
I played (a feature) that I played a lead

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.630
in like this.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:36.940
But for me I’m just kind of realizing it
now, now it’s out and people are watching

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.170
it.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:43.630
Because until then you are just so caught
up in all of the having to get it finished,

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.260
having to get it made.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:52.430
And someone was saying to me at like every
level 50 million, 100 million, there are always

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.410
problems, you know from the producing side.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:58.110
There are always things that come up and you
just have to learn to deal with them.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:03.440
For me now, I’ve learned so much on this
film, like hopefully the next one is going

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.200
to be a little bit easier because I think
I’ve faced every problem you can face in

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.650
making a film.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.780
And you can get through it and I think as
long as you have the passion and determination

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.840
and you don’t give up, it’s worth it.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.840
It is worth it.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.840
I now have a film.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.000
Film Courage: Which has distribution.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.020
Nadia Jordan: Yes, which has distribution
and people are watching.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.660
And you see the reviews come out and it’s
like “Something I made that people are actually

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.340
enjoying it.”

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.760
Because you get so caught up in trying to
make it good and the sound and the edit, the

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.340
distribution and everything that goes with
it, you kind of forget what drove you to make

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.440
it in the first place and the whole vision.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.069
And seeing in the end that people are actually
enjoying it, it’s a really good feeling.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:48.440
Film Courage: Have you always been a finisher?

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.879
You know how some people start many things
and then they leave them half, sort of undone

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:54.680
(for various reasons).

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.150
Do you always make sure you finish everything?

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.940
Nadia Jordan: I mean…I’m not someone who
starts loads of different things.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.129
I have to be really passionate about something
to actually get so involved in it.

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:11.100
But if I am, then I will always finish it.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.550
I always want to get going with things.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:17.489
If I have to sit and read a contract…like
for example I hate board games where I have

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.489
to sit and read the rules.

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:20.069
Let’s just make them up as we go along.

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:22.389
I don’t want to sit there and read the whole
rule book.

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:23.479
Let’s just make them up.

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:26.430
And that’s the kind of thing I just blag
it.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.479
But if it’s something I’m really passionate
about, I’ll always see through it.

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:36.389
I hate not having an ending and having something
half-done.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:41.009
I want to do it whole-heartedly, if it’s
something I actually want to do.

